---
import StudyLayout from '../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro';
---
<StudyLayout
  title="Why we are doing this at home, and how"
  eyebrow="One page for the family"
  lead="Molly is taking three OCR A levels and Functional Skills Maths Level 2 at home. The exams, the papers and the certificates are exactly the ones a college student sits. What is different is the plan behind them, and this page sets it out."
  current="why"
>
  <div class="wrap why">
    <section class="why__s">
      <h2>What she is taking and when</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>OCR Classical Civilisation (H408)</strong>, <strong>OCR Ancient History (H407)</strong> and <strong>OCR Religious Studies (H573)</strong>: exams in May and June 2028, sat at an approved exam centre as a private candidate. All three are 100% written exam. There is no coursework in any of them, so nothing depends on a school before exam day.</li>
        <li><strong>Functional Skills Maths Level 2</strong>: sat online from home with a live invigilator, first attempt in June 2027, with a retake window before September. It is the grade 4 GCSE equivalent that colleges, apprenticeships and employers ask for. Level 1 is banked first in February if the autumn goes slowly.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="why__s">
      <h2>This is a full course, not a hope</h2>
      <p>Everything is written down, dated, and online at this site. In outline:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>63 teaching weeks</strong> from 5 October 2026, about 22 hours a week, with a fixed daily timetable. Each A level gets six to six and a half hours a week. A college timetables about four and a half.</li>
        <li><strong>Every reading, every essay and every test</strong> is planned to the week: which books of Homer, which chapters of Thucydides, which OCR past paper, which Hodder chapter. The "Today" page shows the day's work with a tick box and a link to where the resource is.</li>
        <li><strong>Assessment on a fixed rhythm</strong>: a timed, marked piece of exam writing every Friday; ten milestone tests per subject across the two years; a full mock exam series in January 2028 and again in April 2028; a maths check every Friday and three maths mocks before the real one. Every mark and action point is logged.</li>
        <li><strong>Marking that is honest.</strong> Everything is marked with OCR's own mark schemes and level grids. Twice a year an examiner-tutor marks one essay per subject independently, so home marking is checked against the real standard.</li>
        <li><strong>The primary texts first.</strong> The Bloomsbury and Hodder textbooks are the OCR-endorsed ones, but the plan is built around reading the actual sources, which is where the marks are.</li>
        <li><strong>Maths taught the way the last five years were not.</strong> Thirty minutes a day, one idea, real objects before any symbols, one method per topic, and 28 lesson pages written for exactly this learner: a picture, hands-on first, the same steps every time, worked examples with every key press, and a checked video. Traditional teaching failed her on abstraction; this plan never starts with the abstract.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="why__s">
      <h2>Compared with college and with an online course</h2>
      <p>To be fair to both: a college gives other students, a teacher who has marked for the board, and fixed deadlines. An online course gives a textbook, a tutor who marks a handful of assignments per subject, and leaves you to find an exam centre yourself. Here is what this plan does about each.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Teacher marking</strong>: replaced by OCR's published mark schemes, examiners' reports for every paper since 2019, and the twice-yearly external marker.</li>
        <li><strong>Deadlines</strong>: the Friday timed piece, the milestone dates and the two mock series are fixed in the calendar and tracked with scores.</li>
        <li><strong>Pace</strong>: a college moves at the class's speed; this plan is reviewed at the end of every term and adjusted for one student, with a rule that writing practice is never the thing cut.</li>
        <li><strong>Other students</strong>: home-educated A level students study these exact OCR subjects in online groups and forums, where they swap essays, compare marks and talk through the set texts in the same way students do on an Open University or school platform. Molly will join those for her subjects, and a study group or a tutor for any one subject is there if she wants it.</li>
        <li><strong>Choice of subjects</strong>: this exact combination is rarely available at one college. Choosing the options so that the same ancient texts count for two exams roughly halves the reading load.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="why__s">
      <h2>Exams, costs and logistics</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Private candidates are normal. JCQ runs a public finder for centres that accept them. We contact three centres in September 2027 and confirm entries by the OCR deadline of about 21 February 2028.</li>
        <li>Cost of the three A levels: about £900 to £1,200 including centre fees. Maths: about £160 to £200 per attempt, booked two weeks ahead, retakes allowed once results are back.</li>
        <li>Access arrangements (extra time) are applied for through the centre, with evidence gathered early if needed.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="why__s">
      <h2>What "ready" means, and what happens if she is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maths</strong>: the exam is booked only when a full mock scores 55% or more; the pass mark is about 58% and the plan aims at 70%. If she fails, the result is not certificated and she retakes within the twelve months.</li>
        <li><strong>A levels</strong>: the January 2028 mocks are marked against OCR grade boundaries. If a subject is clearly not on track, we say so in January, and the choice is to enter anyway, to drop to two subjects, or to enter that subject in summer 2029. Nothing is entered on hope.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="why__s">
      <h2>How you can see it working</h2>
      <p>The whole plan, the calendar, the day-by-day page and every test score are on this site, and the ticks sync so we all see the same page.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="why__foot"><a href="/study/">The full plan</a> · <a href="/study/calendar/">The calendar</a> · <a href="/study/today/">Today</a> · <button type="button" class="cmd__link" onclick="window.print()">Print this page</button></p>
  </div>
</StudyLayout>

